[{:registry/operating-states [:intake :propose :approve :execute :audit], :actor/wave 2, :registry/required-technologies [:robotics :identity :audit-ledger], :actor/scope-excluded-ops [:use-force :authorize-use-of-force :deploy-weapon :discharge-weapon :fire-weapon :make-arrest :authorize-arrest :detain-suspect :authorize-detention :authorize-search :authorize-seizure :conduct-search :pursue-suspect :engage-suspect :authorize-pursuit], :actor/robotics? true, :adr/related ["adr-2790003355-cloud-itonami-isco-3355-police-inspectors-detectives-coverage"], :adr/status :accepted, :actor/scope-excluded-terms ["use force" "used force" "apply force" "applied force" "authorize the use of force" "authorized the use of force" "authorize use of force" "authorized use of force" "deploy the weapon" "deployed the weapon" "discharge the weapon" "discharged the weapon" "fire the weapon" "fired the weapon" "make the arrest" "made the arrest" "authorize the arrest" "authorized the arrest" "execute the arrest" "executed the arrest" "place under arrest" "placed under arrest" "detain the suspect" "detained the suspect" "authorize the detention" "authorized the detention" "take into custody" "taken into custody" "authorize the search" "authorized the search" "conduct the search" "conducted the search" "execute the search warrant" "executed the search warrant" "authorize the seizure" "authorized the seizure" "pursue the suspect" "pursued the suspect" "authorize the pursuit" "authorized the pursuit" "engage the suspect" "engaged the suspect" "実力を行使した" "武器を使用した" "武器を発砲した" "逮捕した" "逮捕を執行した" "拘束した" "身柄を拘束した" "捜索を許可した" "差押えを許可した" "容疑者を追跡した" "容疑者と交戦した"], :adr/id 2792005412, :phase/rollout "{0 {:label \"read-only\", :writes #{}, :auto #{}}, 1 {:label \"assisted-logging\", :writes #{:log-equipment-record}, :auto #{}}, 2 {:label \"assisted-coordination\", :writes #{:log-equipment-record :schedule-patrol-operation :coordinate-supply-order}, :auto #{}}, 3 {:label \"supervised-auto\", :writes #{:log-equipment-record :schedule-patrol-operation :coordinate-supply-order :flag-precinct-concern}, :auto #{:log-equipment-record :schedule-patrol-operation :coordinate-supply-order}}}", :actor/domain :public-safety/precinct-equipment-and-patrol-logistics-coordination, :actor/proposal-ops [:log-equipment-record :schedule-patrol-operation :flag-precinct-concern :coordinate-supply-order], :actor/hard-checks [:officer-verified :effect-propose :op-not-allowed :equipment-unit-basis :tactical-assessment-forbidden :tactical-dispatch-forbidden :weapon-procurement-forbidden :scope-exclusion], :registry/maturity :implemented, :actor/isco-08 "5412", :actor/always-escalate-ops [:flag-precinct-concern], :adr/doc_type "adr", :actor/weapon-item-terms ["firearm" "firearms" "pistol" "pistols" "handgun" "handguns" "rifle" "rifles" "shotgun" "shotguns" "ammunition" "ammo" "bullet" "bullets" "cartridge" "cartridges" "taser" "tasers" "conducted energy weapon" "stun gun" "stun guns" "pepper spray" "oc spray" "chemical irritant" "tear gas" "baton" "batons" "expandable baton" "less-lethal launcher" "less lethal launcher" "beanbag round" "beanbag rounds" "rubber bullet" "rubber bullets" "explosive" "explosives" "flashbang" "flashbangs" "stun grenade" "stun grenades" "grenade launcher" "拳銃" "小銃" "散弾銃" "弾薬" "実弾" "テーザー" "スタンガン" "警棒" "催涙スプレー" "催涙ガス" "手榴弾" "閃光弾"], :operation/nodes [:intake :advise :govern :decide :request-approval :commit :hold], :operation/entry-point :intake, :adr/body "# ADR 2792005412: cloud-itonami ISCO-08 5412 Police Officers precinct/equipment/patrol-scheduling administrative-logistics coordination coverage\n\n## Status\n\nAccepted\n\n## Context\n\nThis is part of the occupation-classification track (sibling to the\n`cloud-itonami-isic-*` industry-actor rollout, same governing\nADR-2607121000): codify every ISCO-08 occupation unit group as an\nautonomous \"actor\" (LLM/advisor behind an independent Governor, a\n`langgraph.graph` StateGraph, append-only audit ledger), modeled on a\nrobotics premise -- a robot performs the occupation's physical/\nadministrative work, proposing actions to an independent Governor that\ngates them, never dispatches hardware itself, always escalates\nhigh-stakes decisions to a human. This ADR covers **ISCO-08 class 5412\n-- Police Officers** only.\n\nThe `kotoba-lang/occupation` registry's live `{:id \"5412\" ...}` entry\nwas verified against a fresh clone before any work began (`clojure -M\n-e \"(require 'kotoba.occupation) (println (kotoba.occupation/get-\noccupation \\\"5412\\\"))\"` from a checkout with the `kotoba-lang/\ntechnology` sibling present at the correct `../technology` relative\npath), and its `:name` read exactly \"Police Officers\" -- confirming\nthe premise, no mismatch. Prior to this ADR the entry's `:maturity` was\n`:spec` with `:required-technologies [:robotics :identity\n:audit-ledger]` and no `:repo`/`:business-id`; `wave-of` resolves this\nentry to rollout wave 2. No repository existed at\n`cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-5412` (`gh api` 404 confirmed before\nscaffolding). This ADR and its companion registry edit promote the\n`5412` entry to `:implemented`.\n\n**This is, alongside the concurrent sibling `cloud-itonami-isco-5413`\n(Prison Guards), likely the single highest-stakes occupation actor\nbuilt in this fleet's session.** Police Officers have direct legal\nauthority to use force (up to and including lethal force in extreme\ncircumstances), carry and use weapons, make arrests, and physically\ndetain individuals -- a domain where wrong autonomous action could\nkill or seriously injure someone, or cause an unlawful deprivation of\nliberty. This ADR applies the same guardrail shape established for the\nfleet's prior most-cautious precedent, **ADR-2607152500** (Wave4\nrollout start amendment: decisions directly touching a person's\nsafety, dignity or subsistence must always exclude the closed op\nallowlist and always escalate to a human, not merely be gated behind a\nconfidence threshold), and mirrors in full the two-layer structural-\nexclusion pattern proven in the concurrent sibling\n`cloud-itonami-isco-3355` (Police Inspectors and Detectives, built\nearlier in this same session at maximum safety rigor for a comparable\ndomain) -- with additional domain-specific defense-in-depth described\nbelow, since this occupation's failure modes (direct physical force,\nlethal weapons) are at least as severe as, and arguably more immediate\nthan, 3355's investigative-authority failure modes.\n\nThe reference implementation mirrored for module SHAPE is\n[`cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-3313`](https://github.com/cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-3313)\n(Accounting Associate Professionals: `store`/`advisor`/`governor`/\n`actor` split, `langgraph.graph` StateGraph, full doc set). The\nreference mirrored for the closed-allowlist + registered-AND-verified\nprovenance + content-based scope-exclusion SAFETY PATTERN specifically\nis [`cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-3355`](https://github.com/cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-3355)\n(Police Inspectors and Detectives) -- read in full in this session,\nadapted substantially to this occupation's use-of-force/weapon/arrest/\ndetention/search/pursuit guardrail described below rather than\nfollowing either reference's domain shape directly.\n\n## Decision\n\nPublish `cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-5412` as an OSS\ndocumentation/logistics-coordination actor for police officers,\nfollowing the SAME governed-actor architecture as every prior\n`cloud-itonami-isco-*` actor in this fleet: **Precinct Coordination\nAdvisor ⊣ PrecinctOpsGovernor** on a `langgraph.graph` StateGraph\n(`io.github.kotoba-lang/langgraph`, git-sha-pinned identically to the\nreference's `deps.edn` -- this is a different library coordinate from\nthe `langgraph-clj` used by the unrelated `20-actors/*` actor family),\nwith an independent Governor, human-in-the-loop approval via\n`interrupt-before`, and an append-only audit ledger.\n\nThis actor is deliberately **precinct/equipment/patrol-scheduling\ndocumentation and administrative-logistics coordination ONLY, never\ntactical or enforcement authority** -- it must NEVER be designed to\nexercise, simulate exercising, or propose exercising ANY of: a\nuse-of-force decision (of any kind, from physical restraint to lethal\nforce), a weapon-deployment decision, an arrest, a detention, a\nsearch/seizure authorization, or a decision to pursue/engage a\nsuspect. These are not merely gated behind escalation -- they are\nstructurally absent from the op-allowlist entirely.\n\n### Closed proposal-op allowlist (all `:effect :propose`)\n\n- `:log-equipment-record` -- non-weapon equipment/vehicle readiness\n  data entry (equipment condition metadata only -- mileage, mechanical\n  condition, inspector, timestamp -- NEVER a tactical assessment)\n- `:schedule-patrol-operation` -- administrative shift/patrol-route\n  rostering proposal (never a real-time tactical dispatch during an\n  active incident)\n- `:flag-precinct-concern` -- surface an administrative/equipment/\n  training concern, ALWAYS escalates immediately, NEVER\n  auto-commit-eligible\n- `:coordinate-supply-order` -- non-weapon equipment procurement\n  proposal (vehicles, radios, protective gear -- NEVER weapons/\n  ammunition)\n\nThis actor has **NO op, anywhere in its allowlist, that resembles**: a\nuse-of-force decision, a weapon-deployment decision, an arrest, a\ndetention, a search/seizure authorization, or a decision to\npursue/engage a suspect. These are **structurally absent from the\nop-allowlist entirely**, not merely gated behind escalation -- an op\nsuch as `:use-force` or `:make-arrest` cannot even be proposed by the\nreference mock advisor (its `infer` function only ever emits one of\nthe four allowlist ops, driven by a `case` expression with no default\nbranch producing any other op shape), and if any code path (a forged\nproposal, a future LLM advisor, a misbehaving fork) ever produced such\nan op, the governor's closed-allowlist HARD check (`:op-not-allowed`)\nrejects it unconditionally and permanently. Verified two ways: (a)\nunit tests directly against `governor/check` for fifteen\nconcretely-named forbidden op fixtures spanning all six forbidden\ncategories (`precinctops.governor/scope-excluded-ops`: `:use-force`,\n`:authorize-use-of-force`, `:deploy-weapon`, `:discharge-weapon`,\n`:fire-weapon`, `:make-arrest`, `:authorize-arrest`,\n`:detain-suspect`, `:authorize-detention`, `:authorize-search`,\n`:authorize-seizure`, `:conduct-search`, `:pursue-suspect`,\n`:engage-suspect`, `:authorize-pursuit`), and (b) an END-TO-END test\nthat drives each of those same fifteen ops through a ROGUE advisor and\nthe FULL `langgraph.graph` actor graph (not just the governor's pure\ncheck function), proving the StateGraph itself always resolves to\n`:hold` with zero records committed even when the advisor node itself\nis compromised\n(`precinctops.actor-test/holds-every-scope-excluded-op-attempt-even-via-a-rogue-advisor`).\nA companion end-to-end test also proves a rogue advisor that forges\n`:effect` away from `:propose` (attempting direct actuation/dispatch)\nis held before any record commits\n(`precinctops.actor-test/holds-a-forged-direct-actuation-attempt-even-via-a-rogue-advisor`).\n\n### Eight HARD governor checks (permanent, un-overridable)\n\n1. **Officer unregistered/unverified** -- any proposal must resolve to\n   an independently `:registered?`/`:verified?` officer record. Never\n   trusts the proposal's own claim of who the officer is. This directly\n   implements the task's \"officer/precinct record must be independently\n   verified/registered before any action\" invariant -- the officer\n   record itself carries `:precinct-id`, so officer verification and\n   precinct affiliation are checked together, not as two separate\n   entity lookups.\n2. **Effect not `:propose`** -- any other `:effect` value is a HARD\n   block (a claim to directly actuate outside governance).\n3. **Op not allowed** -- any `:op` outside the closed four-op allowlist\n   is a HARD, PERMANENT block. This independently rejects any attempt\n   to slip in a `:use-force`/`:deploy-weapon`/`:make-arrest`/\n   `:detain-suspect`/`:authorize-search`/`:pursue-suspect`-shaped op.\n4. **Equipment-unit basis** -- `:log-equipment-record`,\n   `:schedule-patrol-operation` and `:coordinate-supply-order` must\n   cite a REGISTERED AND VERIFIED equipment unit belonging to the\n   officer's own precinct (`:missing-equipment-id` /\n   `:unknown-equipment` / `:equipment-unverified` /\n   `:equipment-wrong-precinct`). `:flag-precinct-concern` is\n   deliberately EXEMPT from this check -- it is the channel by which\n   any administrative/equipment/training concern (including one with\n   no specific equipment reference) is surfaced for human intake, so\n   it must not be blocked on an equipment record it may not need.\n5. **Tactical-assessment forbidden** -- `:log-equipment-record` is\n   equipment/vehicle condition metadata ONLY. Any proposal carrying a\n   tactical-assessment field (`:threat-level`, `:tactical-assessment`,\n   `:suspect-location`, `:incident-severity`, `:engagement-status`,\n   `:use-of-force-assessment`, `:suspect-description`) is a HARD,\n   PERMANENT block -- this actor never records a tactical or incident\n   assessment, only equipment condition. This check has no equivalent\n   in the `cloud-itonami-isco-3355` reference and was added because\n   this domain's \"documentation\" op (an equipment log) is a\n   plausible smuggling vector for an incident/tactical observation in\n   a way the reference's case-file log was not.\n6. **Tactical-dispatch forbidden** -- `:schedule-patrol-operation` is\n   administrative shift/patrol-route rostering ONLY, never a real-time\n   tactical dispatch during an active incident. Any proposal carrying\n   active-incident/dispatch content fields (`:active-incident-id`,\n   `:dispatch-order`, `:tactical-directive`,\n   `:pursuit-authorization`, `:engage-target`, `:real-time-dispatch`,\n   `:suspect-description`) is a HARD, PERMANENT block -- this actor\n   never dispatches or directs a live tactical response, only routine\n   administrative rostering. This is the single most important\n   domain-specific guardrail in this ADR: an administrative\n   \"scheduling\" op is exactly the kind of op a naive design could\n   accidentally widen into real-time tactical dispatch during an\n   active incident, which this check makes structurally impossible\n   regardless of what an advisor (or a compromised advisor) proposes.\n7. **Weapon-procurement forbidden** -- `:coordinate-supply-order` may\n   only name non-weapon equipment (vehicles, radios, protective gear).\n   Any proposal whose `:item` or `:category` names a weapon,\n   ammunition, or other lethal/less-lethal-equipment procurement item\n   (`precinctops.governor/weapon-item-terms` -- firearms, ammunition,\n   tasers/conducted-energy weapons, pepper spray/chemical irritants,\n   batons, less-lethal launchers, explosives/flashbangs, plus Japanese\n   equivalents) is a HARD, PERMANENT block -- independent of and IN\n   ADDITION TO the cost-threshold escalation (below); a weapon or\n   ammunition item is blocked even at a cost of $1. This is a\n   dedicated content-based check (`precinctops.governor/weapon-item?`)\n   distinct from the free-text scope-exclusion check (#8) because it\n   inspects a structured field (`:item`/`:category`) that a naive\n   design might otherwise treat as routine procurement data merely\n   subject to a cost ceiling -- this ADR's task guidance explicitly\n   required this be a hard block, \"not just gate it.\"\n8. **Scope exclusion** -- independent of the op-allowlist check and\n   checks 5/6/7, any proposal whose free text (`:rationale` or\n   `:note`) names a finalization/execution action for a use-of-force\n   decision, a weapon deployment, an arrest, a detention, a\n   search/seizure authorization, or a pursuit/engagement of a suspect\n   (e.g. \"use force\", \"deploy the weapon\", \"make the arrest\",\n   \"detain the suspect\", \"authorize the search\", \"pursue the\n   suspect\", plus Japanese equivalents) is a HARD, PERMANENT block,\n   evaluated unconditionally on content. This is defense-in-depth on\n   top of checks 3/5/6/7: even if a future change somehow widened the\n   allowlist or an allowed op's field shape, content-based rejection\n   still independently blocks tactical/enforcement language wherever\n   it appears in free text.\n\n### One always-escalate (SOFT) gate, one threshold gate, plus the general confidence floor\n\n- `:flag-precinct-concern` -- ALWAYS escalates to a human, regardless\n  of confidence (governor's `always-escalate-ops`). This is the ONLY\n  channel by which the robot may surface an observation suggesting a\n  precinct/equipment/training concern needs human officer/supervisor\n  attention -- it never resolves the observation itself, and the\n  robot's role ends at \"here is the flagged concern,\" never \"here\n  is what enforcement action to take.\"\n- a `:coordinate-supply-order` proposal whose `:cost` exceeds\n  `supply-cost-escalation-threshold` (500, inclusive boundary verified\n  by `ok-at-exact-supply-cost-threshold-boundary`) -- always needs\n  human sign-off, independent of confidence, for ALLOWED (non-weapon)\n  items only. This is an ESCALATION, not a hard block: an over-budget\n  vehicle/radio/protective-gear order is not itself unsafe, unlike a\n  weapon/ammunition procurement attempt, which is HARD-blocked\n  regardless of cost by check 7 above -- the two mechanisms\n  deliberately differ because the underlying risk differs (budget\n  overrun vs. structurally forbidden capability).\n- (advisor confidence below `confidence-floor` 0.6 also escalates, for\n  any op.)\n\n### Self-trip guard (fleet-known bug class, carried over from the sibling ISIC/ISCO tracks)\n\nA governor's scope-exclusion term list phrased as a bare noun (e.g.\n\"force\", \"weapon\", \"arrest\") can accidentally match inside the\nmock advisor's own default rationale text for a legitimate proposal,\nself-blocking the actor's own happy path -- this is exactly the risk\nthis vertical's own vocabulary (every proposal necessarily discusses\nofficer/equipment/precinct concepts to do its documentation job) could\ntrigger if phrased carelessly. `precinctops.governor/scope-excluded-\nterms` is phrased exclusively as finalization/execution ACTIONS (e.g.\n\"use force\", \"deploy the weapon\", \"make the arrest\", \"detain\nthe suspect\", \"authorize the search\", \"pursue the suspect\"),\nnever bare nouns, and `precinctops.governor-test/default-mock-advisor-\nproposals-never-self-trip-scope-exclusion` is a dedicated regression\ntest asserting every op in the closed allowlist (including both the\nequipment-id-present and equipment-id-nil variants of\n`:flag-precinct-concern`), driven through the default mock advisor,\nnever trips `:scope-excluded`, `:op-not-allowed`, or\n`:weapon-procurement-forbidden`. The mock advisor's own default\nrationale template (\"documented <op> for equipment <id>\" /\n\"documented <op> (no equipment reference -- precinct-level\nconcern)\") was written to avoid any bare-noun collision by\nconstruction, and the test fixtures' default `:item` values (e.g.\n\"replacement tires\", \"reflective safety vest\") were chosen to\navoid any accidental collision with `weapon-item-terms`. Green on\nfirst run in this session -- no self-trip was actually hit during\ndevelopment, but this ADR still documents the guard and its regression\ntest since the risk class is especially acute for this domain's\nvocabulary.\n\n## Consequences\n\n- `kotoba-lang/occupation` registry's `{:id \"5412\" ...}` entry\n  promoted from `:spec` to `:implemented`, with `:repo`/`:business-id`\n  set to the real repository\n  (`https://github.com/cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-5412` /\n  `cloud-itonami-isco-5412`), and `maturity-summary`'s implemented\n  count bumped accordingly (recomputed live from the registry via\n  `kotoba.occupation/maturity-summary`, not assumed, immediately before\n  the edit to account for concurrent sibling landings in this same\n  batch -- see the companion `occupation_test.clj` edit for the exact\n  live-recomputed counts).\n- Full test suite (`clojure -M:test`): 54 tests, 203 assertions, 0\n  failures, 0 errors -- raw output captured in this ADR's\n  `:test/counts` and in the task report, independently re-verified\n  against a brand-new fresh clone of the pushed repository (not just\n  the local working copy), with a byte-level UTF-8 mojibake check on\n  the Japanese scope-exclusion/weapon-item term lists (0 mojibake\n  found).\n- No `manifest/west.yml` changes -- this actor is registered purely via\n  the `kotoba-lang/occupation` capability registry, consistent with\n  sibling `cloud-itonami-isco-*`/`cloud-itonami-isic-*` actors.\n- No `kotoba-lang/technology` dependency was added to the actor's\n  `deps.edn` -- consistent with the live convention already confirmed\n  by sibling ISCO actors (only the central `kotoba-lang/occupation`\n  registry itself depends on it, to resolve `:required-technologies`\n  stacks).\n- This ADR is EDN-only, matching the live `90-docs/adr` convention\n  (commit `6d2d0eeda658`, \"Remove all .md, normalize ADRs to\n  tx-data\") -- a single-entity EDN vector with an `:adr/body` string\n  blob rather than a paired `.md`+`.edn`.\n\n## Alternatives considered\n\n- Giving the actor an op like `:approve-use-of-force`/\n  `:authorize-arrest` that is merely gated behind\n  `always-escalate-ops` (the shape `cloud-itonami-isco-3313` uses for\n  `:approve-over-ceiling-posting`, or the shape a naive reading of\n  \"gate high-stakes actions behind human approval\" might suggest):\n  rejected outright per the explicit task guidance, the\n  `ADR-2607152500` precedent, and the `cloud-itonami-isco-3355`\n  precedent -- an always-escalate op still means the *capability* to\n  use force/deploy a weapon/make an arrest/detain a person/authorize a\n  search/pursue a suspect exists in the code path and is merely\n  deferred to a human gate. For decisions this severe (potential loss\n  of life, irreversible physical harm, unlawful deprivation of\n  liberty), the capability itself must not exist. The only op related\n  to escalation is `:flag-precinct-concern`, which surfaces the need\n  for human attention and never itself decides, authorizes, or defers\n  a tactical/enforcement action for later automatic execution.\n- A bare-noun scope-exclusion term list (e.g. `[\"force\" \"weapon\"\n  \"arrest\" \"detain\" \"search\" \"pursue\"]`): rejected outright per\n  the fleet-known self-trip bug class described above -- this actor's\n  own domain necessarily discusses officer/equipment/precinct concepts\n  to do its documentation job, so a bare-noun list would risk\n  self-blocking the actor's own legitimate proposals (e.g. logging\n  equipment \"issued for patrol\" or a supply item like a \"duty\n  belt\" could plausibly collide with careless bare-noun terms; the\n  actual implementation avoids this by using full finalization/\n  execution action phrases for `scope-excluded-terms`, and a narrowly\n  scoped, explicitly weapon-specific term list for\n  `weapon-item-terms`).\n- Merging `officer` and `equipment` into a single entity (only\n  checking one provenance record): rejected -- the cross-precinct\n  check (`:equipment-wrong-precinct`) requires two independently\n  registered-AND-verified entities so that an equipment unit belonging\n  to a different precinct than the proposing officer is caught even if\n  both records individually pass verification, matching the\n  two-entity provenance shape already established by the\n  `cloud-itonami-isco-3355` reference's officer+case pattern.\n- Gating `:coordinate-supply-order` weapon items ONLY behind the cost\n  threshold (treating a cheap weapon/ammunition item as auto-commit-\n  eligible): rejected outright per the explicit task guidance\n  (\"must additionally hard-block any weapon/ammunition/lethal-\n  equipment procurement item, not just gate it\") -- cost is\n  orthogonal to the risk a weapon procurement represents; a $1\n  ammunition order is exactly as structurally forbidden as a $50,000\n  one, which is why check 7 is a dedicated, cost-independent HARD\n  block rather than a lower auto-commit ceiling.\n- Allowing `:schedule-patrol-operation` to reference a live incident\n  ID purely for informational/read-only purposes (no dispatch\n  authority, just a pointer): considered, but rejected for this\n  initial implementation -- even a read-only incident reference is a\n  plausible seed for scope creep into real tactical coordination over\n  time (a \"linked incident\" field today, a \"dispatch priority\"\n  field tomorrow). Administrative rostering only for v1, with zero\n  incident-linkage fields; a follow-up ADR could reintroduce a\n  narrowly-scoped, explicitly non-tactical incident-reference field if\n  a legitimate administrative need is demonstrated, with its own\n  dedicated forbidden-keys guardrail.\n- A numeric confidence-only gate for `:log-equipment-record` /\n  `:schedule-patrol-operation` (no forbidden-keys content check):\n  rejected as insufficient -- confidence measures the advisor's\n  certainty about the *proposal it chose to make*, not whether that\n  proposal smuggles forbidden content; a high-confidence advisor could\n  still misuse an allowed op's field shape to carry a forbidden\n  tactical assessment or dispatch directive, which is exactly why\n  checks 5/6 exist as independent, content-based, permanent HARD\n  blocks regardless of confidence.\n\n## Itemized safety confirmation\n\nEvery op in this actor's closed proposal-op allowlist, and why it is\nsafe under the constraints above:\n\n1. **`:log-equipment-record`** -- non-weapon equipment/vehicle\n   condition metadata only (condition, mileage, inspected-by,\n   timestamp). Cannot carry a tactical or incident assessment\n   (HARD-blocked by check 5, `:tactical-assessment-forbidden`, on a\n   closed forbidden-key list: `:threat-level`, `:tactical-assessment`,\n   `:suspect-location`, `:incident-severity`, `:engagement-status`,\n   `:use-of-force-assessment`, `:suspect-description`). Not a\n   use-of-force decision, weapon deployment, arrest, detention,\n   search/seizure authorization, or pursuit/engagement in any respect\n   -- it is a records-management action indistinguishable in kind from\n   a fleet manager's vehicle-inspection log.\n2. **`:schedule-patrol-operation`** -- administrative rostering\n   logistics only (shift start/end, route/beat). Cannot carry a\n   real-time tactical dispatch (HARD-blocked by check 6,\n   `:tactical-dispatch-forbidden`, on a closed forbidden-key list:\n   `:active-incident-id`, `:dispatch-order`, `:tactical-directive`,\n   `:pursuit-authorization`, `:engage-target`, `:real-time-dispatch`,\n   `:suspect-description`). Does not dispatch, direct, or authorize any\n   live tactical response -- it is a calendar/roster action.\n3. **`:flag-precinct-concern`** -- surfaces an administrative/\n   equipment/training concern to a human officer/supervisor; ALWAYS\n   escalates (governor's `always-escalate-ops`, unconditionally, at\n   every confidence level), NEVER auto-commit eligible. Carries only\n   administrative trigger metadata (`:reason` from a closed keyword\n   set, `:note` free text subject to the same scope-exclusion check as\n   every other op's free text). This is the ONLY channel through which\n   an observation may be surfaced, and it explicitly never resolves,\n   decides, or authorizes any enforcement action itself -- the human\n   reviews and acts entirely on their own authority.\n4. **`:coordinate-supply-order`** -- non-weapon equipment procurement\n   proposal (item, cost, vendor, category). Cannot name a weapon,\n   ammunition, or other lethal/less-lethal-equipment item (HARD-\n   blocked by check 7, `:weapon-procurement-forbidden`, regardless of\n   cost). Not an enforcement or tactical action of any kind -- it is a\n   purchasing-logistics action for vehicles/radios/protective gear,\n   gated by ordinary cost-ceiling escalation (above 500 always\n   escalates) like any office/fleet procurement workflow.\n\nNo op in this allowlist, and no op anywhere in this codebase, resembles\na use-of-force decision (of any kind, from physical restraint to\nlethal force), a weapon-deployment decision, an arrest, a detention, a\nsearch/seizure authorization, or a decision to pursue/engage a suspect\n-- these six categories are structurally absent, not gated, confirmed\nby: (a) the closed four-op allowlist itself\n(`precinctops.governor/closed-op-allowlist`), (b) fifteen concretely-\nnamed forbidden-op fixtures spanning all six categories that are\nrejected at both the governor-unit level and the full-actor-graph\nlevel via a rogue advisor (`precinctops.governor/scope-excluded-ops`,\n`precinctops.governor-test/every-scope-excluded-op-name-is-rejected`,\n`precinctops.actor-test/holds-every-scope-excluded-op-attempt-even-via-a-rogue-advisor`),\n(c) a content-based scope-exclusion check independent of the `:op`\nfield (`precinctops.governor/out-of-scope?`,\n`precinctops.governor/scope-excluded-terms`), (d) a dedicated\ncontent-based weapon-procurement check independent of both (b) and (c)\n(`precinctops.governor/weapon-item?`,\n`precinctops.governor/weapon-item-terms`), and (e) two additional\ncontent-shape guardrails unique to this domain preventing an\notherwise-allowed op from smuggling forbidden substance through its\nown field shape (checks 5/6 above). Every one of (b) through (e) is\nproven not just at the pure-function `governor/check` level but through\nthe FULL compiled `langgraph.graph` StateGraph driven by a rogue\nadvisor, so even a compromised advisor node cannot reach `:commit` for\nany forbidden action.\n", :adr/date "2026-07-17", :actor/cost-threshold-